"""Landlords & Rentals toolkit: rent ledger, receipt book, expense and repair log, PG/hostel bed register,
   lease starting draft, move-in/move-out checklist and the online rent receipt maker (India HRA mode + any country)."""

KITS = [
    dict(id='rentals', name='Landlords & Rentals', icon='🏠', color='#C2410C',
         title='Landlord Toolkit: Rent Ledger, Rent Receipt and Lease Templates for Excel and Word',
         desc='Free landlord templates: rent ledger for many properties, rent receipt book, expense and repair log, PG bed register, lease draft, inspection checklist and online rent receipts.',
         intro=['Letting out a flat, a few shops or a PG means the same paperwork every month: who has paid, who is behind, which deposit you are holding, what the plumber cost, and a receipt the tenant can give to their employer. Most small landlords keep this in a diary and a WhatsApp chat, and it falls apart at the first dispute.',
                'This toolkit gives you that record as Excel workbooks that work out arrears, advances, lease end dates and net income by themselves, Word drafts for the lease and the move-in inspection, and an online rent receipt maker. Pick your own currency; everything also works for landlords outside India, and the files stay on your own computer.'],
         who='Individual landlords, property managers, PG and hostel owners, housing society secretaries and tenants who need rent receipts.'),
]

ITEMS = [
    dict(id='rent-ledger-excel', kit='rentals', kind='excel', icon='🏘️', name='Rent Ledger for Multiple Properties (Excel)',
         title='Rent Ledger in Excel for Multiple Properties: Tenant Rent Tracker with Arrears',
         desc='Free Excel rent tracker for landlords: every unit and tenant, rent due and paid month by month, arrears or advance, deposits held, lease end alerts and reminders.',
         files=[('Rentals-Rent-Ledger.xlsx', 'Rent ledger workbook')],
         preview={'Rentals-Rent-Ledger.xlsx': ('Ledger', 'A4:R9')},
         lead='One workbook for every flat, shop or room you let out, across several buildings. List each unit with its tenant, rent, deposit and lease dates, then write down payments as they come in. The ledger works out how many months of rent have fallen due, what has been paid, the arrears or advance for each tenant, a 12-month paid grid with red gaps, and how many days are left on each lease.',
         inside=['Settings: your name, currency symbol, the first month of the ledger year, the "as on" date and your list of properties',
                 'Units sheet for up to 200 units: unit code, property, flat or shop number, tenant, phone, monthly rent, deposit held, rent due day, lease start and end, arrears brought forward',
                 'Payments sheet with 1,000 rows: date paid, unit (drop-down), the month the rent is for, amount, mode and reference; tenant and property fill in by themselves',
                 'Ledger sheet: months due, rent due, paid, balance, status (Arrears, Advance, Paid up), deposit, days left on the lease and a 12-month grid that turns red for unpaid months and green for paid ones',
                 'A ready reminder message for each tenant who is behind, to copy into WhatsApp, SMS or email',
                 'Summary by property: units, occupied, monthly rent roll, rent due so far, collected, arrears, deposits held and money received this month'],
         steps=['Open the Settings sheet. Type your name, your currency symbol (₹, $, £, €, AED…), the first month of the ledger year (for example 01-04-2026 for an Indian financial year or 01-01-2026 for a calendar year) and your property names.',
                'On the Units sheet, replace the sample rows. Give every unit a short unique code such as GV-101, pick its property, and type the tenant, monthly rent, deposit, rent due day (for example 5) and lease dates. Leave the tenant empty for a vacant unit.',
                'If a tenant already owed rent before the ledger year, type that amount in "Arrears brought forward".',
                'Every time rent comes in, add a row on the Payments sheet: date, unit code, the month the rent is for (any date in that month) and the amount. A part payment is just a row with a smaller amount.',
                'Open the Ledger sheet. Each unit shows rent due up to the "as on" date, the amount paid and the balance. Red months in the grid are unpaid or short; green months are paid in full.',
                'Copy the reminder message of any tenant in arrears and send it. Check the Summary sheet for each building\'s totals.',
                'At the end of the year, save a copy for the new year, set the new start month, type each tenant\'s closing balance as "Arrears brought forward" and clear the Payments sheet.'],
         tips=['A month counts as due once its rent due day has passed. With a due day of 5, rent for this month is not shown as due until the 5th.',
               'Advance rent shows as a negative balance with the status "Advance". A tenant who pays three months ahead simply has three rows on the Payments sheet, one per month.',
               'Leases ending within 60 days turn orange in the "Days left" column and ended leases turn red, so you have time to renew or find a new tenant.',
               'Dates follow your computer\'s regional setting (for example 05-04-2026 or 4/5/2026), so the file reads naturally in any country.',
               'Keep the security deposit out of rent: it is shown separately so it is never counted as income by mistake.'],
         faqs=[('How is the arrears figure worked out?', 'Arrears brought forward, plus monthly rent multiplied by the months that have fallen due in the ledger year (from the later of the ledger start and the lease start, up to the "as on" month and not past the lease end), minus all payments recorded for that unit up to the last month of the year.'),
               ('Can I use it for shops and offices as well as flats?', 'Yes. A unit can be anything with a monthly rent: a flat, a shop, an office, a room, a parking space or a godown. Use the Property column to group them by building.'),
               ('What if the rent goes up in the middle of the year?', 'The ledger multiplies one monthly rent by the months due. When rent changes, type the new rent and add the difference for the earlier months to "Arrears brought forward" (it can be negative), or start the new rent from the next ledger year.'),
               ('Does it handle GST on commercial rent or TDS?', 'No. It records rent received. If you charge GST on commercial rent or your tenant deducts tax at source, record the amount actually received and keep the tax working with your accountant.'),
               ('Does it work in Google Sheets or LibreOffice?', 'Yes. It uses SUMIFS, EDATE, INDEX and MATCH, which work in Google Sheets and LibreOffice Calc, along with the drop-down lists and colour rules.')]),

    dict(id='rent-receipt-book-excel', kit='rentals', kind='excel', icon='🧾', name='Rent Receipt Book (Excel)',
         title='Rent Receipt Format in Excel: Printable Rent Receipt Book with Amount in Words',
         desc='Free Excel rent receipt book: keep a register of receipts, pick a receipt number and print tenant and landlord copies with amount in words, PAN line and stamp note.',
         files=[('Rentals-Rent-Receipt-Book.xlsx', 'Rent receipt book workbook')],
         preview={'Rentals-Rent-Receipt-Book.xlsx': ('Receipt', 'A1:F21')},
         lead='The paper rent receipt book, but every receipt is numbered, nothing is lost and the amount in words is never misspelt. Enter each rent payment as a line in the register; then pick a receipt number and print an A4 page with a tenant copy and a landlord copy. It writes the amount in words in Indian (lakh, crore) or international (million) style with your own currency name, shows the landlord PAN line for Indian HRA claims and reminds you when a revenue stamp is usually expected.',
         inside=['Settings: landlord name, address, phone, PAN or tax ID, currency symbol and name, words style (Indian or International) and country (India or other)',
                 'Register sheet with 500 rows: receipt number, date, tenant, property address, rent period from and to, amount, payment mode and reference',
                 'Receipt sheet: pick a receipt number and get a tenant copy and a landlord copy on one A4 page, with "Received with thanks" wording, amount in figures and words, period, mode and signature box',
                 'India mode: landlord PAN line, a warning when yearly rent is above ₹1,00,000 and PAN is missing, and a revenue stamp box on cash receipts above ₹5,000',
                 'Yearly total per tenant on the Register sheet, useful for HRA proof or a landlord\'s own income records'],
         steps=['On the Settings sheet type the landlord\'s name, address and phone. In India add the landlord\'s PAN; elsewhere you may add a tax ID or leave it empty.',
                'Set the currency symbol and its name in words (for example ₹ and Rupees, $ and Dollars, £ and Pounds), the minor unit (Paise, Cents, Pence) and the words style.',
                'On the Register sheet add one row per receipt: number (R-001, R-002…), date, tenant, property address, the period the rent covers, amount, mode (cash, UPI, bank transfer, cheque) and reference.',
                'Go to the Receipt sheet and pick the receipt number from the list in the yellow cell. Both copies fill in.',
                'Print the page (File › Print, A4), cut along the dotted line, sign both copies and give the tenant copy to the tenant. Paste a revenue stamp first if the box asks for one.',
                'For the next receipt, add a line on the Register sheet and pick its number.'],
         tips=['Never reuse a receipt number. If a receipt is wrong, write "Cancelled" in its Notes column and issue a new number.',
               'Employers in India usually ask for the landlord\'s PAN when the rent paid in the year is more than ₹1,00,000; the Settings sheet warns you when the register shows that much rent without a PAN.',
               'Many employers ask for a ₹1 revenue stamp signed across on cash rent receipts above ₹5,000. Payments by bank or UPI already leave a record; mention the reference.',
               'One receipt can cover several months (for example April to June). Most employers accept monthly or quarterly receipts; ask yours.',
               'HRA exemption is available only under the old tax regime in India. Ask your employer or tax adviser what proof they need for your year.'],
         faqs=[('Who should make the rent receipt, tenant or landlord?', 'The landlord issues and signs it, because it confirms that the landlord received the money. In practice tenants often prepare the receipts and ask the landlord to sign; this workbook suits both.'),
               ('Is a rent receipt enough to claim HRA?', 'Employers usually ask for rent receipts and, when the yearly rent is above ₹1,00,000, the landlord\'s PAN. Some also ask for the rent agreement or bank statements. The rules are set by the income tax law and your employer\'s process, so check with them.'),
               ('Can I use it outside India?', 'Yes. Set the country to Other, your currency symbol and name, and the International words style. The PAN line and revenue stamp box then disappear.'),
               ('Can I add my own logo or signature image?', 'Yes. Unprotect the Receipt sheet (Review › Unprotect Sheet), insert the picture in the header area and protect it again.')]),

    dict(id='rent-expense-log-excel', kit='rentals', kind='excel', icon='🔧', name='Property Expense & Maintenance Log (Excel)',
         title='Property Expense Tracker in Excel: Rental Maintenance Log and Net Income',
         desc='Free Excel log for landlords: property expenses by category, repair requests with status and days open, costs to recover from tenants, and net income per property.',
         files=[('Rentals-Property-Expense-Log.xlsx', 'Property expense and maintenance workbook')],
         preview={'Rentals-Property-Expense-Log.xlsx': ('Summary', 'A3:G12')},
         lead='Rent is only half the story: property tax, society charges, a leaking tap, a fresh coat of paint. Record every expense against its property and category, keep a list of repair requests with who is fixing them and how long they have been open, and see each property\'s yearly expenses and net income next to the rent you received.',
         inside=['Settings: currency symbol, year start, your properties and an editable list of 16 expense categories (repairs, plumbing, electrical, painting, property tax, society or HOA fees, insurance, loan interest and more)',
                 'Expenses sheet with 1,000 rows: date, property, unit, category, description, vendor, amount, bill number and "Charge to" (owner or tenant)',
                 'Repairs sheet: date reported, property, unit, reported by, problem, priority, assigned to, status, date fixed and cost; days open count by themselves and jobs open longer than 7 days turn red',
                 'Summary: the year\'s expenses by category and by property, amounts to recover from tenants, open repair jobs, rent received (you type it) and net income per property'],
         steps=['On the Settings sheet type your currency symbol, the first day of your accounting year and your property names. Edit the category list if you need different heads.',
                'Each time you pay for something, add a row on the Expenses sheet with the property, category and amount. Choose "Tenant" in "Charge to" if the tenant must pay it back (for example damage beyond normal wear).',
                'When a tenant reports a problem, add it on the Repairs sheet with a priority and who will fix it. Update the status and the date fixed when it is done, and add the cost on the Expenses sheet too.',
                'On the Summary sheet type the rent received from each property this year (copy it from the Rent Ledger workbook). Net income works out by itself.',
                'Keep the bills: scan or photograph them and note the bill number in the row, so you can find them at tax time.'],
         tips=['Use the same property names in this workbook and in the Rent Ledger so totals match.',
               'Big improvements (a new roof, a new lift) are treated differently from repairs by tax rules in many countries; tag them clearly in the description and ask your accountant.',
               'In India, house property income gets a standard deduction for repairs, so many owners cannot claim actual repair costs separately; this log is still useful for your own records and for deposit deductions. Check the rules for your country.',
               'Review the Repairs sheet every week: a small leak left for a month becomes a big bill.'],
         faqs=[('Which expenses count as "Charge to: Tenant"?', 'Whatever your agreement says the tenant pays, such as damage beyond normal wear and tear, the tenant\'s share of a bill, or utilities you paid for them. These are totalled separately as "To recover from tenants" and are not counted as your expense.'),
               ('Can I track repairs without recording a cost?', 'Yes. The Repairs sheet is a separate list; the cost column there is for your reference. Only rows on the Expenses sheet are added into the totals.'),
               ('How do I see one property only?', 'Use Data › Filter on the Expenses or Repairs sheet and pick the property, or read its row on the Summary sheet.')]),

    dict(id='rent-pg-bed-register-excel', kit='rentals', kind='excel', icon='🛏️', name='PG & Hostel Bed Register (Excel)',
         title='PG and Hostel Management in Excel: Bed Register, Resident Records and Rent Dues',
         desc='Free Excel register for PG, hostel and co-living owners: beds and rooms, who stays where, vacancies, resident ID and emergency details, monthly rent dues and reminders.',
         files=[('Rentals-PG-Hostel-Bed-Register.xlsx', 'PG and hostel bed register workbook')],
         preview={'Rentals-PG-Hostel-Bed-Register.xlsx': ('Beds', 'A6:H12')},
         lead='A paying guest house or hostel changes residents every month, and the owner must know at once which beds are empty, who sleeps where, who has paid and how to reach a resident\'s family in an emergency. This register keeps beds, residents and rent payments in three simple lists, then shows occupancy, vacant beds and each resident\'s dues automatically.',
         inside=['Settings: PG or hostel name, currency symbol, "as on" date, rent due day and lists for food plans and ID types',
                 'Beds sheet for 200 beds: bed code, room, floor, sharing, AC, rent per bed; the current resident and Occupied or Vacant status fill in from the resident list, with totals and occupancy percentage at the top',
                 'Residents sheet for 500 residents: phone, ID type and number, emergency contact, home address, college or workplace, bed, rent, check-in and check-out dates, deposit, food plan, police verification status and extra charges',
                 'Payments sheet: date, resident, month the rent is for, amount, mode and reference',
                 'Dues sheet: months due, rent due, paid, balance and status for every resident, with a reminder message ready to send'],
         steps=['On the Settings sheet type your PG or hostel name, your currency symbol and the rent due day.',
                'On the Beds sheet list every bed with a short code (for example 101-A, 101-B), its room, sharing type and rent.',
                'When a resident moves in, add them on the Residents sheet with their bed, check-in date, rent, deposit and ID details. When they leave, type the check-out date: the bed becomes vacant on the Beds sheet.',
                'Record each payment on the Payments sheet with the month it is for.',
                'Open the Dues sheet to see who owes what, and copy the reminder for anyone in arrears.',
                'For electricity, food or a part-month stay, type the extra (or a negative adjustment) in the "Extra charges / adjustment" column of that resident.'],
         tips=['Two residents must never have the same bed at the same time; if the Beds sheet shows the wrong name, check for an old resident without a check-out date.',
               'Many Indian cities expect PG and hostel owners to get residents\' police verification done and to keep a copy of their ID; the Police verification column helps you track it. Check your local rules.',
               'Store ID numbers only as long as you need them, keep the file password-protected (File › Info › Protect Workbook) and never share the Residents sheet.',
               'The first month is counted in full from the check-in month. For a pro-rata first month, enter a negative adjustment for the days not stayed.'],
         faqs=[('Can I use it for a hostel with dormitory beds?', 'Yes. Each bed is one row, whatever the room size. A 6-bed dormitory is six rows with the same room number.'),
               ('How is the monthly rent due counted?', 'From the check-in month up to the current month (once the rent due day has passed), or up to the check-out month for residents who have left, multiplied by the resident\'s rent, plus any extra charges or adjustments.'),
               ('What if a resident changes beds?', 'Type a check-out date on the old row and add a new row for the same person with the new bed and the next check-in date. Use the same name with a small difference (for example "Riya Shah (2)") so payments go to the right row.'),
               ('Is the resident data safe?', 'It stays in the file on your computer. Protect the file with a password and limit who can open it, because it holds personal details.')]),

    dict(id='rent-lease-agreement-word', kit='rentals', kind='word', icon='📜', name='Rent Agreement / Lease Starting Draft (Word)',
         title='Rent Agreement Format in Word: Residential Lease Starting Draft to Edit',
         desc='Free editable rent agreement and lease draft for Word: parties, property, rent, deposit, term, maintenance, notice, inventory schedule and India notes. Check local law first.',
         files=[('Rentals-Rent-Agreement-Draft.docx', 'Rent agreement / lease draft (Word)')],
         lead='A plain-English residential rent agreement to start from, so you do not begin with a blank page or a photocopy of someone else\'s agreement. It covers the parties, the property, rent and due date, security deposit and its return, the term and renewal, use of the property, repairs, utilities, entry by the landlord, notice and ending the tenancy, plus an inventory schedule and signature blocks. It is a starting draft only: tenancy law, stamp duty and registration differ by country, state and city, so have it checked before anyone signs.',
         inside=['A clear "starting draft, check local law" box at the top that you delete once a professional has reviewed it',
                 '20 numbered clauses in plain English with [brackets] for every detail you must fill: rent, due day, late fee, deposit, lock-in, notice period, rent increase, utilities, pets, subletting',
                 'Schedule 1: description of the property; Schedule 2: fittings and furniture inventory table with condition',
                 'Signature blocks for landlord, tenant and two witnesses',
                 'Notes for India: the 11-month practice, stamp duty, registration, police verification and the Model Tenancy Act'],
         steps=['Download the file and open it in Word, Google Docs or LibreOffice Writer.',
                'Read the whole draft once. Delete clauses that do not fit your situation and add any that your local law or society rules require.',
                'Replace every [bracketed] item. Use Find (Ctrl + F) and search for "[" until none are left.',
                'Fill Schedule 2 with every item of furniture and every appliance, with its condition. Use the Move-in / Move-out Checklist in this toolkit for the detailed room-by-room record.',
                'Have the draft checked by a lawyer or a local agent who knows the tenancy rules where the property is, then print it on the right stamp paper or e-stamp if your state requires it.',
                'Both parties sign every page; the witnesses sign at the end. Register it where the law requires and give the tenant a copy.'],
         tips=['Write the deposit amount, the rent and the notice period both in figures and in words.',
               'Agree in writing how the deposit will be returned and within how many days of the keys being handed back.',
               'Attach copies of both parties\' ID and the property\'s ownership proof if your local practice requires it.',
               'In India many agreements are made for 11 months because a lease from year to year or for more than one year must be registered; stamp duty still applies to the 11-month agreement and its amount depends on the state.'],
         faqs=[('Is this a legally valid agreement?', 'It is a starting draft written in common, simple terms. Whether an agreement is valid and enforceable depends on the law where the property is: stamp duty, registration, notice periods and deposit limits all vary. Have it checked by a professional before signing.'),
               ('Why do Indian rent agreements run for 11 months?', 'Under the Registration Act, a lease from year to year or for a term of more than one year must be registered, which means a visit to the sub-registrar and higher costs. An agreement for 11 months avoids that requirement, though it still needs the correct stamp duty.'),
               ('What is the Model Tenancy Act?', 'A model law released by the Government of India in 2021 for states to adopt. Where a state has adopted it in some form, there can be rules on the security deposit cap, a written agreement and informing a Rent Authority. Check whether your state follows it.'),
               ('Can I use this outside India?', 'Yes. The clauses are written for any country; delete the India notes at the end and follow your local rules on deposits, notice and registration.')]),

    dict(id='rent-inspection-checklist-word', kit='rentals', kind='word', icon='📋', name='Move-in / Move-out Inspection Checklist (Word)',
         title='Move-in and Move-out Inspection Checklist for Rentals: Free Word Template',
         desc='Free rental inspection checklist for Word: room-by-room condition at move-in and move-out, meter readings, keys, inventory and a deposit deduction summary to sign.',
         files=[('Rentals-Move-In-Move-Out-Checklist.docx', 'Move-in / move-out checklist (Word)')],
         lead='Most deposit disputes come down to one question: what condition was the home in when the tenant moved in? Walk through the property with this checklist on the first day and again on the last, write the condition of every wall, floor, fitting and appliance side by side, note the meter readings and keys, and agree any deposit deductions in writing.',
         inside=['Details of the property, landlord, tenant and the two inspection dates',
                 'Room-by-room tables (entrance, living room, kitchen, two bedrooms, two bathrooms, balcony, outside and parking) with condition at move-in, condition at move-out and photo reference',
                 'Meter readings for electricity, water and gas at move-in and move-out',
                 'Keys, remotes and access cards handed over and returned',
                 'Deposit settlement: deposit held, itemised deductions, amount returned and signatures'],
         steps=['Print the checklist (or fill it on a laptop or tablet) and walk through the property with the tenant on move-in day.',
                'For every item write a short condition such as "Good", "Scratch on left door", "Tap drips", and take a photo; note the photo number in the last column.',
                'Write the meter readings and list every key and remote handed over. Both sign the move-in section and keep a copy each.',
                'On move-out day, walk through again with the same copy and fill the move-out column.',
                'Agree deductions for damage beyond normal wear and tear in the deposit settlement table, sign it and return the balance as agreed.'],
         tips=['Photos with the date visible are the best evidence; keep them in one folder named after the property and the date.',
               'Normal wear and tear (faded paint after years, worn carpet in walkways) is usually the landlord\'s cost, not the tenant\'s. Agree what counts as damage before moving in.',
               'Add or delete rows for rooms your property does not have; a studio needs fewer tables than a three-bedroom house.'],
         faqs=[('Who should keep the checklist?', 'Both. Make two copies signed by both landlord and tenant at move-in, and use the same copies again at move-out so the two columns are side by side.'),
               ('Can a landlord deduct cleaning costs from the deposit?', 'It depends on your agreement and local law. Many places allow deductions for leaving the property dirtier than it was received, but not for normal wear and tear. Write the agreed rule into the rent agreement.'),
               ('Does this work for furnished and unfurnished homes?', 'Yes. For a furnished home, list every piece of furniture and appliance in the inventory rows and in Schedule 2 of the rent agreement.')]),

    dict(id='rent-receipt-maker', kit='rentals', kind='online', tool='rent-receipt', icon='⚡', name='Online Rent Receipt Maker',
         title='Online Rent Receipt Generator: HRA Rent Receipts with PAN, Print or Download',
         desc='Make rent receipts online for one or many months: landlord PAN for HRA, revenue stamp note, amount in words, any currency. Print, save as PDF or download Excel and Word.',
         lead='Make a signed-ready rent receipt for one month or a whole year in a minute. Type the tenant, landlord and property once (they stay saved on this device), add the months with the rent paid, and get a receipt with the total in figures and words. In India mode it adds the landlord PAN line that employers ask for when rent is more than ₹1,00,000 a year and a revenue stamp note for cash; switch to another country to use your own currency and date format.',
         inside=['India (HRA) mode with landlord PAN and revenue stamp reminders, or any other country with 15 currencies and three date formats',
                 'One line per month with rent, date paid, payment mode and reference; one receipt for the whole period',
                 'Total in figures and in words (Indian lakh/crore style or international style)',
                 '"Received with thanks" statement, property address and signature lines for landlord and tenant',
                 'Print / Save as PDF, download as Excel or Word; nothing is uploaded'],
         steps=['Choose the country mode: India (HRA receipt) or Other country. For other countries also pick your currency and date format.',
                'Type the tenant\'s name, the landlord\'s name and address, the landlord\'s PAN (India) and the rented property\'s address. These stay saved on this device.',
                'Type the receipt number and date.',
                'Add one line per month: the month, the rent, the date it was paid and how (cash, UPI, bank transfer, cheque). Use "Add month" for more.',
                'Read the notes under the receipt: they tell you if a PAN or a revenue stamp is usually needed.',
                'Press Print / PDF, or download the receipt as Excel or Word. Get it signed by the landlord.'],
         tips=['For HRA, many employers accept one receipt per quarter or per year; ask yours. Make monthly receipts if they want them.',
               'Rent paid by bank transfer or UPI is easy to prove: type the reference in each line.',
               'If the rent is more than ₹50,000 a month, the tenant may have to deduct TDS on rent in India; check the current rules with a tax adviser.',
               'Only make receipts for rent actually paid. A false rent receipt can lead to tax penalties.'],
         faqs=[('Is a landlord PAN compulsory?', 'In India, employers usually ask for the landlord\'s PAN when the rent paid in the year is more than ₹1,00,000. Below that, it is not usually needed. If the landlord has no PAN, employers typically ask for a declaration from the landlord.'),
               ('Is a revenue stamp needed on a rent receipt?', 'Many employers ask for a ₹1 revenue stamp, signed across, on rent receipts above ₹5,000 paid in cash. The receipt shows a reminder when that applies. For payments by bank or UPI, the transaction reference is usually enough.'),
               ('Can I use it for a shop or office rent?', 'Yes. The receipt works for any rent. For commercial rent with GST, issue a proper tax invoice instead, for example with the GST Invoice Maker in the Accountants toolkit.'),
               ('Is my data sent anywhere?', 'No. The receipt is made in your browser. Names and addresses are saved only in this browser on this device; clearing the site data removes them.')]),
]
